  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:R\\to R$ which is given by $f(x)=(x-2)^{2}+1?$\n\n- The range of $f$ is $[1,\\infty )$\n- $\u2200\\,y\\in R,$ $\u2203\\,x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=y$\n- $f$ is **onto**",
  "worked_solution": "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14101062/assets/bed7b280998fe987276ca5820b987f672404d52d00f532db819db1166118ed70.png)\n\nA function $f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection (also known as *onto*) if, for *every* element $y$  in $Y,$ there is *at least one* element in $X$ that maps to $y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection if $\u2200\\,y\\in Y,$ $\u2203\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, the lowest $y$-value is $1,$ and it occurs at the point $(2,1).$ The graph will cover all $y$-values from $y=1$ and greater, including the number $1,$ so the range of the function is $[1,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II is false. Consider $y=0.$ As we can see from the range, there is no real number $x$ such that $f(x)=0.$ So, it's *not true* that for all $y$ contained within $R,$ there exists some $x$ contained within $R$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, $f$ is not a surjection of $R$ onto $R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
